## std::vector

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>   // for sort/reverse
using namespace std;
```

**Declaring**
```cpp
vector<int> v1;                    // empty
vector<int> v2(5);                 // 5 zeros
vector<int> v3(5, 9);              // 5 nines
vector<int> v4 = {10, 20, 30};      // initializer list
```

**Adding / removing**
```cpp
vector<int> v;
v.push_back(10);   // {10}
v.push_back(20);   // {10, 20}
v.pop_back();       // {10}
```

**Access**
```cpp
vector<int> v = {5, 10, 15};
cout << v[0];        // 5 — no bounds check
cout << v.at(1);      // 10 — throws if out of range
cout << v.front();   // 5
cout << v.back();    // 15
cout << v.size();    // 3
```

**Looping**
```cpp
vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};

for (int i = 0; i < v.size(); i++)   // index-based
    cout << v[i] << " ";

for (int x : v)                       // range-based
    cout << x << " ";

for (auto x : v)                      // same, auto type
    cout << x << " ";
```

**Insert / erase**
```cpp
vector<int> v = {10, 20, 40, 50};
v.insert(v.begin() + 2, 30);   // {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}
v.erase(v.begin() + 1);         // remove index 1
```

**Clear / empty check**
```cpp
v.clear();
cout << v.empty();   // 1 (true)
```

**Sorting & reversing**
```cpp
vector<int> v = {5, 2, 8, 1};
sort(v.begin(), v.end());          // ascending: {1,2,5,8}
sort(v.rbegin(), v.rend());        // descending: {8,5,2,1}
reverse(v.begin(), v.end());        // just flips current order
```

---

## One-glance rule to remember

`[]` is fast but does **no bounds checking** — going out of range is undefined behavior.
`.at()` is a touch slower but **throws an exception** if you go out of range — safer while debugging.
This applies to both `string` and `vector`.
